\begin{abstract}
The \software{hep-cv} package and class provide a compact $\CV$ layout for academic applications.
The package defines the left-column $\CV$ line and entry layout, list styling, month helpers, and bibliography support, while the class adds article-based $\CV$ defaults, contact and photo title pages, cover-letter helpers, section and page styles, and subfile support.
\end{abstract}

\newgeometry{vscale=.8, vmarginratio=3:4, includeheadfoot, left=11em, marginparwidth=4.6cm, marginparsep=3mm, right=7em}

\section{Introduction}

The \software{hep-cv} package enables the user to write an appealing \CV.
The style is heavily influenced by the \software{moderncv} package \cite{moderncv}.
In order to use the package layout macros the user has to load only the \software{hep-cv} package in addition to the "article" class.
\begin{verbatim}
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{hep-cv}
\end{verbatim}
The source also provides a "hep-cv" class for complete \CV documents and a "hep-cv-bibliography" module for \CV bibliography layouts.

\subsection{Options}

\DescribeMacro{date}
\DescribeMacro{list}
The "date" option switches to a compact \CV date scheme: it defines the month macros described in \cref{sec:months} and makes the bibliography module print months with these macros and years in the short form "'25".
Without it, the usual date scheme is kept.
The "list=false" option prevents the package from adjusting the standard list environments.

\section{Macros}

\DescribeMacro{\setleftwidth}
\DescribeMacro{\setseperatorwidth}
The layout can be tuned by setting the "\setleftwidth" and "\setseperatorwidth" macros.

\DescribeMacro{\cvline}
The "\cvline"\oarg{left}\marg{main} adds an unformatted line to the \CV.

\begin{verbatim}
\cvline[left]{cvline example}
\end{verbatim}

\DescribeMacro{\cventry}
The "\cventry"\oarg{left}\marg{main 1}\oarg{main 2}\meta{*}\oarg{\dots} macro adds a boldface entry to the \CV.
The contents passed to the following arguments alternate between upright and italic text.
Starred optional arguments begin on a new line.
For example:

\begin{verbatim}
\cventry[left]{cventry example}[one][two]*[starred three][four]
\cventry[left]{cventry example}[one]
\cventry[left]{cventry example}
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Lists}

\DescribeEnv{itemize}
The "itemize" environment is adjusted using the "enumitem" package \cite{enumitem}.
\DescribeEnv{enumerate}
The "enumerate" environment is adjusted.
\DescribeEnv{description}
The "description" environment is adjusted.
\DescribeEnv{enumdescript}
The "enumdescript" environment of the "hep-text" package \cite{hep-text} is adjusted.

\subsection{Months} \label{sec:months}

\DescribeMacro{\Jan}
\DescribeMacro{\Feb}
\DescribeMacro{\Mar}
\DescribeMacro{\Apr}
\DescribeMacro{\May}
\DescribeMacro{\Jun}
\DescribeMacro{\Jul}
\DescribeMacro{\Aug}
\DescribeMacro{\Sep}
\DescribeMacro{\Oct}
\DescribeMacro{\Nov}
\DescribeMacro{\Dec}
With the "date" option, the month macros spread a short month name evenly across the width of \enquote{May} in the current font and are intended for compact date columns.
They are robust and print the plain month name in PDF strings such as bookmarks.

\section{Class additions}

The "hep-cv" class is based on "article" and loads the package layout with full-page \CV defaults.

\DescribeMacro{font}
\DescribeMacro{serif}
\DescribeMacro{hyperref}
The class accepts a "font="\meta{size} option (the standard size options "10pt", "11pt" and "12pt" are shortcuts for it), the "serif" option, and a "hyperref" switch that controls whether \software{hep-reference} is loaded for PDF metadata and links.

\DescribeMacro{\address}
\DescribeMacro{\addressfont}
\DescribeMacro{\addcontactline}
The "\address"\marg{text} macro stores contact information, "\addressfont" controls its font, and "\addcontactline"\marg{text} appends additional contact material.

\DescribeMacro{\opening}
\DescribeMacro{\closing}
\DescribeMacro{\makelettertitle}
\DescribeMacro{\makeletterclosing}
\DescribeEnv{letter}
The cover-letter helpers store opening and closing text, typeset a letter title and closing, and provide a "letter" environment that wraps one letter page.

\DescribeMacro{\titlefont}
\DescribeMacro{\infofont}
\DescribeMacro{\addtitleline}
\DescribeMacro{\photo}
\DescribeMacro{\maketitle}
The class title page uses "\author" for the name, optional "\photo"\marg{file} material, "\addtitleline"\marg{label}\marg{text} for title metadata, and "\addcontactline" for contact metadata.
The "\titlefont" and "\infofont" macros control the title and metadata fonts.

\DescribeEnv{abstract}
The class renames the abstract to \enquote{Summary} and formats it in the \CV left-column layout.

\DescribeMacro{\cvpart}
\DescribeMacro{\insubfile}
The "\cvpart"\marg{name} macro stores the current \CV part for headers and PDF metadata, and "\insubfile"\marg{code} emits \meta{code} only when a subfile is compiled on its own with the \software{subfiles} class, \eg "\insubfile{\cvpart{Education}}" in the preamble of a subfile.
When the subfile is included in the main \CV with "\subfile", the code is ignored.

\section{Bibliography}

The bibliography module mirrors the standalone \software{hep-cv-bibliography} package.
It defines \CV date sorting, section-level bibliography headings, left-column date and index helpers, and "unnumbered", "numbered", and "starrednumbered" bibliography environments for publication lists.
The "date" option described above can also be given to the bibliography module, "\usepackage[date]{hep-cv-bibliography}", which passes it on to \software{hep-cv}, also if the "hep-cv" class has already loaded it.

